From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] EE memory: runtime data in kernel RAM, heap up to the main stack

What broke:
  Ben 10 exited through std::terminate after a few thousand frames, while
  loading its first sound bank. Its newlib sbrk asks EndOfHeap for the heap
  limit, and the runtime capped the heap at 0x01F00000: the top megabyte of
  RAM was kept for the runtime's own RPC packet/server pools, TLS blocks,
  boot-mode records and the stacks that callbacks run on. The game needs its
  heap to reach 0x01F87000; at 0x01F00000 sbrk failed, memalign returned NULL,
  a chunk-handler table was copied to address 0 and a stale handler with a
  pure virtual method was called.
  Separately, dispatchSifCommand took its packet buffers from guestMalloc,
  whose first block starts at the game's heap base (0x00285FD0): every SIF
  command delivered to the EE was written over the start of the game's own
  newlib heap.

Why:
  On the PS2, EE RAM 0x00000000-0x0007FFFF is kernel memory. The kernel keeps
  its structures and its stacks there; games are linked at 0x00100000 and
  their heap may grow up to their main thread's stack.

The fix:
  - New ps2_kernel_ram.h: a fixed layout of runtime-owned guest data in
    0x00020000-0x0007FFFF (RPC packet pool, RPC server pool, TLS pool,
    boot-mode pool, a 64-slot SIF command packet pool, 128 KB of callback
    stacks).
  - State.h: the pools use that layout.
  - ps2_runtime: callback stacks are reserved from the kernel RAM window;
    the heap hard limit starts at the end of RAM and SetupThread (stack -1)
    lowers it to the bottom of the main stack (limitGuestHeapBelow).
  - SetupHeap/EndOfHeap: "rest of RAM" defaults to PS2_RAM_SIZE, clamped by
    the limit above.
  - dispatchSifCommand: packets come from the kernel RAM pool; a full pool is
    logged instead of silently dropping the command.
  - ps2xTest GS VSync callback test: the stack check uses the new window.
  SetupThread still returns sp = RAM - size for stack -1 (unchanged), so the
  heap now ends at 0x01FE0000 for a 64 KB main stack.

How it was found:
  gdb on the pure-virtual stub: the handler table base was 0, the lookup read
  entries from guest address 0; a breakpoint on the game's sbrk
  (0x001C54B0) showed the break pointer at exactly 0x01F00000 and failing.
  With the limit raised the game's break peaks at 0x01F87000 and the stop is
  gone. Test: tests/runtime/test_ee_memory_layout.cpp.

diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_kernel_ram.h b/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_kernel_ram.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f27400b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_kernel_ram.h
@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+#pragma once
+
+#include <cstdint>
+
+// Guest memory that belongs to the runtime's EE kernel, not to the game.
+//
+// On the PS2, EE RAM 0x00000000-0x0007FFFF is kernel memory: the kernel keeps
+// its own data and stacks there, and games are linked at 0x00100000 or above.
+// Everything the runtime itself places in guest RAM (RPC packets and server
+// records, TLS blocks, boot-mode records, SIF command packets, the stacks that
+// interrupt handlers and callbacks run on) lives in this window, so none of it
+// can overlap the game's heap. The game's heap may then grow up to the main
+// thread's stack, as it does on the console.
+//
+// Layout (all inside 0x00020000-0x0007FFFF; the low 128 KB stay untouched,
+// they hold the exception-vector area and the syscall table mirror):
+//   0x00020000  RPC packet pool          64 KB
+//   0x00030000  RPC server pool          64 KB
+//   0x00040000  TLS pool                 64 KB
+//   0x00050000  boot-mode pool            4 KB
+//   0x00051000  SIF command packet pool   8 KB (64 x 128 bytes)
+//   0x00060000  callback/interrupt stacks, 128 KB, handed out from the top
+namespace ps2_kernel_ram
+{
+    constexpr uint32_t kBase = 0x00020000u;
+    constexpr uint32_t kEnd = 0x00080000u;
+
+    constexpr uint32_t kRpcPacketPoolBase = 0x00020000u;
+    constexpr uint32_t kRpcPacketPoolBytes = 0x00010000u;
+
+    constexpr uint32_t kRpcServerPoolBase = 0x00030000u;
+    constexpr uint32_t kRpcServerPoolBytes = 0x00010000u;
+
+    constexpr uint32_t kTlsPoolBase = 0x00040000u;
+    constexpr uint32_t kTlsPoolBytes = 0x00010000u;
+
+    constexpr uint32_t kBootModePoolBase = 0x00050000u;
+    constexpr uint32_t kBootModePoolBytes = 0x00001000u;
+
+    // A SIF command packet is at most 112 bytes (sceSifSendCmd limit).
+    constexpr uint32_t kSifCmdPacketPoolBase = 0x00051000u;
+    constexpr uint32_t kSifCmdPacketSlotBytes = 0x80u;
+    constexpr uint32_t kSifCmdPacketSlotCount = 64u;
+
+    constexpr uint32_t kCallbackStackFloor = 0x00060000u;
+    constexpr uint32_t kCallbackStackTop = kEnd;
+
+    static_assert(kRpcPacketPoolBase + kRpcPacketPoolBytes <= kRpcServerPoolBase, "kernel RAM pools overlap");
+    static_assert(kRpcServerPoolBase + kRpcServerPoolBytes <= kTlsPoolBase, "kernel RAM pools overlap");
+    static_assert(kTlsPoolBase + kTlsPoolBytes <= kBootModePoolBase, "kernel RAM pools overlap");
+    static_assert(kBootModePoolBase + kBootModePoolBytes <= kSifCmdPacketPoolBase, "kernel RAM pools overlap");
+    static_assert(kSifCmdPacketPoolBase + kSifCmdPacketSlotBytes * kSifCmdPacketSlotCount <= kCallbackStackFloor,
+                  "kernel RAM pools overlap");
+    static_assert(kCallbackStackTop <= 0x00100000u, "kernel RAM must stay below the game's load address");
+}
